### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

This is Erdős Problem 906, explicitly marked `@[category research open]` in the Formal Conjectures library — it is an unsolved open problem in complex analysis. The conjecture asks whether there exists a non-zero entire transcendental function $f:\mathbb{C}\to\mathbb{C}$ such that for every strictly increasing sequence $(n_k)$ of natural numbers, the set $\{z : \exists k,\, f^{(n_k)}(z)=0\}$ is dense in $\mathbb{C}$.

No proof or disproof is known. Constructing such a function would require simultaneously controlling the zero sets of all iterated derivatives along every subsequence — a requirement far stronger than known constructions (which typically handle a single fixed sequence). Standard tools (Weierstrass products, interpolation, Baire-category arguments) do not appear sufficient to guarantee the "for all subsequences" universal quantifier while keeping $f$ transcendental and entire. Conversely, no obstruction is known that would rule out such a function's existence.

As an open research problem, I cannot provide a complete proof.
